{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And for all their living-creatures.</b> The plain sense of the verse is: for all their needs — except that it should not have been necessary to say this, since it is included within the general category of their property that the verse already mentioned. But our Rabbis of blessed memory in tractate Makkot (Makkot 12a) explained that it comes to say that the cities were given only for the living and not for burial, that one does not bury in them except the manslayer who is included by the pronouncement of HaShem, for it says (Numbers 35:25): כי שם תהיה קבורתו. According to their words of blessed memory, the word ״ולכל״ is difficult, since the phrase ״ולכל חיתם״ came only to exclude burial of the dead — ״לחיתם״ is what it should have said, for ״ולכל׻ implies an expansion regarding uses of the pasture-land, and all the more so with the added vav: ״ולכל חיתם׻ is an expansion. Rather, it intended ״ולכל׻ as an expansion, and they understood that the exclusion is derived from the word ״חיתם׻ itself. Or, because the intent was to exclude burial, it was necessary to include everything else besides it, and this is the measurement of the verse: <b>ולכל</b> type of use that is for the living. From this you learn that it excluded only burial, for had it said ״לחיתם׻ it would have been understood to exclude everything that is not entirely for the needs of their living, and other things besides burial would have entered the category of prohibition."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:17:18.508508581Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}